#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * RETIRED. `docs/reference/endpoint-workflow-ids.json` is now generated by
 * `scripts/reconcile-endpoint-register.mjs` from the tenant's own endpoint register.
 *
 * This script built that file by stitching together everything the repository happened to
 * hold — the internal flow register, a portal id file derived from run records, and a table of
 * ids read once out of a deployed config and marked, honestly, "not independently
 * corroborated". It was the best available answer while there was no authority to ask, and it
 * carried its uncertainty in the output rather than hiding it: `portalDiscrepancies: 3`, a
 * `note` on every uncorroborated id, a `source` on every entry.
 *
 * `docs/reference/endpoint-register.json` is that authority. It is exported from the tenant,
 * covers all 25 contract keys, and is post-rotation. Reconciled against it, **every one of the
 * 25 keys this script emitted pointed at the wrong workflow** — including all seven of the
 * hardcoded `DEPLOYED` ids below and all five `PORTAL_DEPLOYED` ones. The estate had moved on.
 *
 * Two generators writing one file is how that file goes stale in the first place, so this one
 * stops rather than competing. The tables it hardcoded are deliberately not carried forward:
 * they are a snapshot of a superseded estate, and reviving them would reintroduce exactly the
 * drift the register exists to end.
 */

console.error(`
  ✖  scripts/build-endpoint-workflow-ids.mjs is retired.

     docs/reference/endpoint-workflow-ids.json is generated from the tenant's
     endpoint register, which is the authority for every workflow id:

         npm run reconcile              # regenerate
         npm run reconcile -- --check   # fail if it has drifted

     This script derived ids from the repository's own older artefacts. Reconciled
     against docs/reference/endpoint-register.json, all 25 keys it produced pointed
     at superseded workflows.
`);
process.exit(2);
